ain. So maybe not. Playing T2 might not be ideal if I want to form that.\n\n3. Play F2: I have only one F2. Playing it would remove the only one. Since there are J2:3 left, maybe not critical. But F2 is my seat wind (East), but the prevailing wind is South. So if I have a Pung of F2 (my seat wind), that would give 2 points (Seat Wind). But since I only have one, maybe not. Playing F2 might be safe.\n\n4. Play W8: W8 is a single. If I play it, others might take it for a Chi. But W8 is in the Character suit. There's no indication others are forming Chows with W8. Maybe safer to play.\n\n5. Play B3: I have two B3s. Playing one might help if I can form a Pung later. But there are B3:2 left. If I play one, others might take it. But if I keep both, maybe I can form a Pung if another B3 comes up.\n\n6. Play T3: Similar to T2. Part of a possible T2-T3-T4 Chi. But again, the T4 is already in the Chi. So playing T3 might not be ideal.\n\n7. Play B7: Single. Maybe part of a pair? B7 and B9 are terminals. If I play B7, maybe someone can take it for a Chi, but B7 is in the Dots suit. Not sure.\n\n8. Play B9: Same as B7. Terminal tile.\n\nLooking at my hand, I have a Chi in T4-T5-T6. If I can form another Chi in the same suit, that could help. For example, T1-T2-T3. But I have T2 and T3. If I have T1, but I don't. The unshown T1 count is 3. If I can get a T1, then T1-T2-T3. But I don't have T1. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe focus on forming Pungs. I have three W3s. If I can get another W3, that's a Pung. But there are 3 left. So if I play a W3 now, others might take it. But if I keep them, maybe I can form a Pung or Kong. Also, if I have a Pung of W3 (my seat wind is East, but W3 is a Character tile, not a wind. Wait, W is Characters. The winds are F1-F4. So W3 is a Character tile, not a wind. So Seat Wind is East (F1). So a Pung of F1 would give 2 points. But I have F2, which is South Wind? No, F1 is East, F2 is South, F3 is West, F4 is North. So my seat wind is East (F1