g at the valid actions again. The best move is probably to play a tile that's not critical for forming melds. For example, playing F2. Since I only have one, and it's a single, and it's not part of any meld. Playing F2 would leave me with more flexibility. Alternatively, playing W8, which is a single. But maybe F2 is better because it's a wind tile. However, my seat wind is East (F1), and I have F2 (South Wind). The prevailing wind is South (F2). So if I have a Pung of F2, that would give 2 points (Prevalent Wind). But I only have one. So if I play F2, I lose that chance. But since I have only one, it's not a Pung. So maybe it's safe to play F2.\n\nAlternatively, playing W3. If I play one W3, I still have two left. If someone else discards a W3, I can Peng. But since I have two, maybe it's better to keep them for a Pung. However, if I play a W3 now, others might take it, but if I keep all three, I can form a Pung later. But since I have three in hand, if I can draw another, that would make four (a Kong). But there are three left. So if I play one, there are three left, so possible to draw.\n\nWait, the unshown W3 count is 3. So if I play one, there are still 3 left. So if I keep all three, I can try to draw a fourth. But if I play one, others might take it for a Peng, which would remove one from the unshown count. So maybe better to keep all three in hand.\n\nSo between playing W3 and F2. If I play W3, I have two left. If I play F2, I lose that tile. Considering that forming a Pung of W3 could be valuable (since it's a Pung, which is worth points), maybe it's better to keep the W3s and play F2. Because F2 is a single and not part of any potential meld except a Pung, which I can't form. So playing F2 is safer.\n\nAnother angle: The player across has a Pung of T6 and a Chi of T7-T8-T9. The next player has a Kong of W9. The previous player has a Chi of W3-W4-W5. So maybe W4 and W5 are already in use. If I have W3s, maybe they can form a Pung if I get another. So playin