ung, that's a Dragon Pung if it's a dragon tile, but W3 is not a dragon. So a Pung of W3 would not be a Dragon Pung. So it might not contribute much unless it's part of another pattern.\n\nAlternatively, if I can form a Pure Shifted Pungs (four pungs in the same suit with consecutive numbers). But I only have one Pung of W3. Not enough.\n\nHmm. Maybe the best approach is to form a Pung of W3s and then find another way to make the rest of the hand. Let's see:\n\nIf I keep all three W3s, then I can form a Pung. Then the remaining tiles are W1, W2, B3\u00d72, B7, J2\u00d72. The J2 pair is the only possible pair. Then I need two more melds. Let's see:\n\n- W1 and W2: can't form a meld unless there's a W3. But I already used the W3 in the Pung. So that's not possible. So maybe I need to discard one of these tiles.\n\nIf I discard W2, then I have W1 and three W3s. Then maybe I can form a Chow of W1, W2, W3 (but I discarded W2). So that's not possible. Alternatively, if I discard W1, then I have W2 and three W3s. Then maybe a Chow of W2, W3, W4 (if I can get a W4). But I don't have a W4. Hmm.\n\nThis seems tricky. Maybe I should discard a tile that's less useful. For example, B7 is a single tile and a terminal. Discarding B7 might be safe. Let's see: If I discard B7, my hand becomes [W1, W2, W3\u00d73, T5, B3\u00d72, J2\u00d72]. Then the melds are [T4-T5-T6], [W3-W3-W3]. The remaining tiles are W1, W2, B3\u00d72, J2\u00d72. The J2 is a pair. Then I need two more melds. W1 and W2 can't form a meld. B3\u00d72: if I can get another B3, that's a Pung. But I only have two. So maybe not. Alternatively, B3 could be part of a Chow if I have B2 and B4. But I don't have those. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W2, then I have W1, three W3s, B3\u00d72, B7, J2\u00d72. The W1 and W3 can be part of a Chow if I have a W2. But I just discarded W2. So maybe others have it. Not sure.\n\nAnother angle: The J2 pair is important. If I discard one J2, then I have only one J2 left, which can't form a pair. So that's bad. So I should keep both J2s.\n\nLooking at the valid actions again: Play W3, W2, J2, W1, B3, B7, T5.\n\nIf I discard B7, that's a terminal tile, which might be less valuable. Also, if I discard B7, I can focus on forming the Pung of W3s and maybe other melds. Let's see if that's the best move.\n\nAlternatively, discarding W3 would reduce the number of W3s to two, which might prevent forming a Pung. But if I can get another W3, but there are three left in the unshown tiles. So maybe it's better to keep the three W3s.\n\nSo the best move might be to discard B7, as it's a single tile and not part of any possible meld. That leaves me with more flexibility for forming the necessary melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for forming a Pung of W3s and a pair of J2s. Discarding B7 (a single tile with no immediate meld potential) preserves flexibility for completing necessary melds.  \nReason: Discarding B7 maintains three W3s for a Pung, keeps the J2 pair intact, and removes a terminal tile that doesn\u2019t contribute to current meld possibilities.  \nAnswer: Play B7"}
